# Model delegation and cost control

The parent handles architecture, planning, coordination, escalation, and final
review. Keep ambiguous diagnosis, architectural choices, and unresolved design
decisions with the parent. Do the implementation through subagents. A longer
or multi-step request is the signal to delegate. Do not wait for the user to
say "use subagents" or name a model.

Once the approach for a part is settled, hand that part to `implementer`.
Hand repetitive, boilerplate, or straightforward parts to `mechanical`. Split
a long task into those parts and launch the independent ones together. After
substantial, architectural, or security-sensitive changes, use `reviewer` for
an independent read-only review; the parent evaluates findings before fixes.

Keep a trivial one-file edit local when starting an agent would dwarf the
change. Give each agent
a small, bounded task, relevant paths and context, concrete existing examples to
follow, acceptance criteria, behaviors to preserve, and required checks. For bug
fixes, require evidence of the cause before edits, rather than speculative patches.
Avoid overlapping file ownership, duplicate exploration, recursive delegation, and
unbounded retries. Stop and escalate after two unsuccessful fix attempts on the
same issue, or immediately when architecture or requirements are unclear. The
parent reassesses the evidence and approach before assigning more work.
The parent remains accountable for integration, verification, and the final result.

Honor user instructions and project-local constraints over these defaults. Agents
must follow the same security, required-reading, protected-file, and consent rules.
Delegation never grants permission to commit, push, deploy, or expand scope.

`implementer` and `mechanical` run on Cursor models (Composer, Grok). On each
launch, pick the most appropriate Cursor model from that turn's available
subagent model list for the task, and pass it as `model`. The model in the
agent file is only a fallback for a launch that omits `model`.

Do not use another model family for these two agents. If the model is rejected,
or the result says the agent ran on a different model, do not keep that result.
Relaunch on another available Cursor model, or tell the user.

`reviewer` is not a Cursor-model agent. Leave its model as configured unless
the user names one.

Model selection is a preference, not a guaranteed spending cap. Keep prompts
and result summaries focused, and verify the model the result reports.

